.Dd June 14, 2026
.Dt MBT 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mbt
.Nd BitTorrent toolkit library
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In mbt/session.h
.In mbt/torrent.h
.In mbt/peer.h
.In mbt/dht.h
.In mbt/metainfo.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a production-grade BitTorrent protocol library written in C.
It implements BEP 3 (core protocol), BEP 5 (DHT), BEP 6 (Fast Extension),
BEP 9 (metadata exchange), BEP 10 (extension protocol), BEP 11 (PEX),
BEP 14 (LSD), and BEP 44 (DHT storage).
.Pp
The library is designed for embedding: it has no global state and no threads.
Dependencies are minimal: a C99 compiler, POSIX sockets, and a SHA-1
implementation.
On Linux, libbsd is required for
.Xr queue 3
macros.
.Ss Architecture
The central object is
.Vt struct mbt_session ,
which owns all other objects:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
A session contains zero or more
.Vt struct mbt_torrent
objects.
.It
Each torrent contains zero or more
.Vt struct mbt_peer
connections.
.It
An optional
.Vt struct mbt_dht
instance provides distributed hash table functionality.
.El
.Ss Event Loop
.Nm
requires an event loop for I/O multiplexing and timers.
The library provides built-in backends for
.Xr sd_event 3
and
.Xr libevent 3 .
A custom backend can be supplied by implementing the
.Vt struct mbt_ev_ops
interface.
.Pp
All library callbacks are dispatched from the event loop.
The library is strictly single-threaded: all API calls and callbacks
execute in the thread running the event loop.
Do not call
.Nm
functions from other threads.
.Ss Object Lifetimes
Pointers returned by accessor functions (e.g.\&
.Fn mbt_session_torrent_first ,
.Fn mbt_torrent_peer_at )
are
.Em borrowed references
owned by their parent object.
They are valid only until the next event loop iteration or until
the parent is modified.
Do not store these pointers across callbacks.
.Pp
If a reference must survive, copy the identifying data
(e.g.\& the infohash) and re-lookup on next use.
.Pp
Objects the caller creates and must free:
.Bl -tag -width "mbt_metainfo" -compact
.It Vt struct mbt_session
Created with
.Fn mbt_session_new ,
freed with
.Fn mbt_session_free .
.It Vt struct mbt_metainfo
Created with
.Fn mbt_metainfo_parse ,
freed with
.Fn mbt_metainfo_free .
.El
.Ss Error Handling
Functions that can fail return a negative errno value on error
and zero or a positive value on success.
.Pp
Callback-based errors (e.g.\& piece hash failure, peer disconnect)
are reported via the session's callback interface, not return values.
.Ss Memory
The library allocates all memory internally via
.Xr malloc 3 .
The caller never frees library-owned memory directly.
Storage backends may allocate their own buffers.
.Ss Storage
Piece data is written and read through a pluggable
.Vt struct mbt_storage_ops
interface.
The built-in
.Fn mbt_storage_file
backend writes to the filesystem.
Custom backends can target any block device or memory buffer.
.Ss Portability
.Nm
runs on Linux, OpenBSD, and FreeBSD.
The hard requirements are:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
C99 compiler
.It
BSD-style sockets (socket, connect, send, recv)
.It
DNS resolution
.Pq Xr getaddrinfo 3
.It
SHA-1 implementation
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Minimal download client:
.Bd -literal
#include <mbt/session.h>

struct mbt_session_config cfg = {
    .storage_ops = &mbt_storage_file_ops,
    .storage_path = ".",
};

struct mbt_session *s = mbt_session_new(&cfg);
mbt_session_enable_lsd(s);
mbt_session_add_magnet(s, "magnet:?xt=urn:btih:...");
mbt_session_run(s);  /* blocks until shutdown */
mbt_session_free(s);
.Ed
